# DTF tier manifest: remote-instance-relay  (artifact-keeper#4776, fixed by #4789, 1.11.3)
# Profile-set: filesystem + a real second Artifact Keeper instance
# (peer.second-instance: `peer-backend` with its own `peer-db`, same image,
# published on 127.0.0.1:${TRIVY_PORT}). Instance A is `backend`; B is the
# remote instance A relays to over the slot's compose network.
#
# THE BUG (#4776)
# The remote-instance relay, GET/POST/PUT/DELETE
# /api/v1/instances/{id}/proxy/{*path} (handlers/remote_instances.rs), lets a
# user reach a registered remote instance with the API key A stores for it.
# Before #4789 it:
#   * dropped the query string (the handlers never read RawQuery), so
#     ?page=, ?per_page=, ?q= and every filter were lost and the remote
#     answered its unfiltered default page;
#   * hard-coded Content-Type: application/json on every POST/PUT body, so a
#     multipart (or any non-JSON) body reached the remote mislabelled;
#   * relayed back only content-type and content-length;
#   * accepted a proxy path under api/v1/instances, so one relay could drive
#     the remote's own instance registry and chain through the remote's
#     relay to a third server.
# #4789 appends the raw query, passes the caller's Content-Type through
# (application/json only when the caller sent none), relays an allow-list of
# response headers (content-disposition, accept-ranges, etag, link,
# x-total-count, ...), and validate_proxy_path refuses api/v1/instances and
# anything under it (lowercased, slashes collapsed) with 400 VALIDATION_ERROR.
# There is no relay header or marker: the refusal is path based, so "a relay
# of a relay" is a proxy path that targets the remote's /api/v1/instances.
#
# THE PEER
# The oracle mints a `*`-scoped API key on B and registers B on A
# (POST /api/v1/instances, url http://peer-backend:8080). It also registers B
# on B itself, so the relay-of-relay chain A -> B's relay -> B has a real
# second hop. Whether a request reached B is read from B's access log
# (api::middleware::tracing logs `uri=<path?query>` per request; the profile
# pins the json-file log driver) and from B's database.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL) true on BOTH images: B's /health direct and relayed agree; a
#             relayed GET without a query returns what B returns; a relayed
#             JSON POST creates a repository on B; B accepts a multipart
#             upload sent directly; a relay path outside api/ and health is
#             refused 400 by the pre-existing guard.
#   (FIX)     RED on 1.11.2:
#             query     relayed GET api/v1/repositories?q=<one key>&per_page=1
#                       returns the same single key and pagination as the
#                       direct call, and B's log shows the query.
#                       1.11.2: all three repos, per_page 20.
#             ctype     relayed multipart POST .../artifacts -> 2xx and B has
#                       the row with the uploaded sha256.
#                       1.11.2: 400 "Invalid `boundary` for multipart/form-data".
#             headers   a relayed download carries B's Content-Disposition and
#                       Accept-Ranges. 1.11.2: both missing.
#             refusal   GET api/v1/instances, a chained
#                       api/v1/instances/{B-self}/proxy/..., a POST to
#                       api/v1/instances, and api//v1/Instances/... are each
#                       400 VALIDATION_ERROR naming api/v1/instances; B's log
#                       records no new /api/v1/instances request and B's
#                       remote_instances table does not grow.
#                       1.11.2: 200 [] / 200 with the chained repo / 200 and a
#                       new row on B.
#
# Teardown note: `harness/run.sh down --slot N` only knows the --<dimension>
# profiles, not this one. A tier run without --keep tears B down itself; after
# --keep, use `docker compose -p ak-dtf<N> -f compose.base.yml
# -f profiles/peer.second-instance.yml down -v`.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: ~30 authenticated calls back to back on A and B.
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: remote-instance URLs go through
# validate_outbound_url (on A, and on B for its self registration) and B sits
# on a private compose address.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem peer.second-instance"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
